This audit wrote only under `results/codex/`. All **291 initially hashed tracked files remained unchanged**, and HEAD remained unchanged: [initial hashes](pass3/input_manifest.json), [source verification](pass3/source_verification.json). Other untracked files appeared during the audit, including `scripts/jobs/lfm25vl450m_l4_v2.json`; this audit did not create or modify them. Findings below concern the tracked Qwen configurations and scripts at the stated commit.

Evidence comes from executed production functions, complete Python scripts with dependency stubs, Jinja renders, synthetic JSONL/pickle/export files, and isolated copies of the actual shell scripts. **No GPU, real model, live VM, network request, package installation, or real upload was used.** Safetensors loading, real Arrow casting, tokenizer-level training masks, GPU capacity, and live Hub/Colab state are not validated. Export fixtures deliberately contain synthetic bytes to test the validator; they are not loadable model positive controls.

All five verification scripts completed their assertions. Some assertions confirm counterexamples, so this does **not** mean all fixes passed. Re-run with:

```sh
python3 -B results/codex/pass3/reproduce.py
```

The runner requires the locally available Jinja2, Pillow, and NumPy packages and creates fresh artifact directories. Successful evidence sets are [logic](pass3/logic_ytabe0j7/results.json), [generation/general evaluation](pass3/generation_v6drr5w7/results.json), [shell/chain](pass3/shell_argr928_/results.json), [template and residual checks](pass3/regressions_naszv_j0/results.json), and [new malformed-action check](pass3/edge_cases_apjhmgt5/results.json). In chain/launcher fixture copies, only the cosmetic stderr version-message filters were removed because the local sandbox rejects their `/dev/fd` process substitution. Production conditions, pipelines, exit handling, and launch commands were preserved. Earlier failed fixture attempts remain in the audit directory; they are not the successful evidence cited here.

| Finding | Status | Reproduction and remaining limitation |
|---|---|---|
| **C1 — completion/artifact identity** | **Partially fixed** | Missing merged weights prevent a new receipt, and absent receipts cause stale `merged`/`lora` directories to be removed before training. But an **empty receipt** or one naming a different model/step makes the shell skip training. The export check accepts one nonempty shard while an index references a missing shard, an empty adapter, and only `tokenizer_config.json`; it also accepts `global_step=1, max_steps=1250`. Existing invalid metrics files still skip scoring. See logic `train_receipt`; shell `empty_receipt`, `mismatched_receipt`, `invalid_cached_metrics`. |
| **C2 — failure propagation** | **Partially fixed** | General-eval, generation, and scorer subprocess failures now produce pipeline exit **1**. A blocking runner with children exiting 0 and 7 exits **1**. Pip exit 17 and an ordinary version mismatch stop bootstrap; failed bootstrap stops fresh-session launch. But all upload subprocesses can exit 17 and the pipeline still prints `PIPELINE DONE`, exit **0**. Chain failures remain unchecked. Version `5.20.20` passes the requested `5.20.2` prefix check; several pinned packages are not checked. See logic `bootstrap`; shell `pipelines`, `blocking_runner`, `chains`. |
| **C3 — frozen GT, prediction identity/resume** | **Partially fixed** | Scoring ignores prediction-supplied labels and joins frozen GT. Missing/extra keys, duplicates, absent generation manifests, and wrong row-model labels each exit **2**. Every tested change to an existing manifest is refused without replacing it. But **GT SHA is never checked**, row `config_hash` is never checked by either consumer, and existing rows without a sidecar acquire a new manifest. A partial Hub restore reproduces the same relabeling and passes scoring. `min_pixels` is omitted from the identity; changing it reuses all rows. Mutable model/data revisions and same-path replacement weights are also absent from the identity. See logic `scorer`; generation `generation` and `restored_stale_rows_score`. |
| **M1 — general-eval pixel mismatch** | **Fixed for fresh runs** | Both current Qwen jobs pass **774400** to base and FT general evaluation, and the evaluator passes the same `size` override to its processor. An explicit `GENERAL_MAX_PIXELS=345600` reaches both sides. Saved summaries record the budget. Reused summaries remain subject to C1. See shell `qwen_argument_checks`; generation `general`; residual checks `resize`. |
| **M2 — matched agent thinking policy** | **Partially fixed** | A third generation call correctly uses FT weights, `--enable_thinking 0 --filter_mode high`, the same AC pixel cap, and the same default 256-token cap as base. Jinja confirms the intended no-think suffix. However, this branch never invokes scoring: it creates `.jsonl.done` immediately after generation. Its high-only output is rejected by the full-GT scorer with exit **2**. See shell `qwen_argument_checks`; logic `scorer.matched_high_only`. |
| **M3 — answer extraction/raw retention** | **Partially fixed** | The old incidental-letter examples now produce C/D, and explicit unfinished `<think>` returns no letter. Complete evaluator runs use **64** new tokens and retain decoded replies longer than 80 characters. But `A. This option is wrong. The answer is D.` still yields **A**, `The answer is B or C.` yields **B**, and `The answer is c` yields lowercase `c`, which is counted as parsed but wrong against uppercase gold. No termination metadata is retained. See logic `templates_and_parser.general_letters`; generation `general`. |
| **M4 — MMLU uncertainty** | **Partially fixed; analysis deferred as stated** | Complete stubbed evaluator runs produce **1,000 MMLU records**, with identical sampled IDs for base and FT. No paired CI or preservation threshold is implemented. Illustratively, 500/1000 has a 95% Wilson interval of **46.91%–53.09%**; this is not a CI for a real run or for a paired accuracy change. Compute paired uncertainty from actual outcomes before claiming preservation. See generation `general`, `mmlu_wilson_500_of_1000`. |
| **M5 — training resume compatibility** | **Partially fixed** | Hub batch-size and positive-`max_steps` mismatches now stop before `trainer.train`. Matching checkpoints resume. A Hub exception still starts fresh training; local checkpoints bypass the checks; changed epoch-derived schedules pass when `max_steps=-1` (the 0.8B recipe). GA/data/template/replay identity is not validated, and `run_config.json` is overwritten first. See logic `train_resume`, `provenance`. |
| **M6 — durable evaluation progress** | **Partially fixed** | At batch size 1, AC uploads occur after rows **40** and **41**; a fresh fixture restores 41 matching rows and generates none. Upload failure still exits **0**, restoration is not atomic, and a torn JSONL line prevents resume. General evaluation still writes nothing if MMLU fails after MMStar completes. See generation `forty_batch_checkpoint`, `fresh_vm_restore`, `upload_failure_ignored`, `torn_jsonl`, `general.crash_after_mmstar`. |
| **M7 — replay provenance/proportion** | **Partially fixed** | Builder writes its actual SHA and requested/kept counts. A stale SHA triggers rebuild. A receipt containing the current SHA but `n_requested=1, n_rows=0, seed=999` and no saved dataset skips rebuilding. Filtering keeps only **4 of 20** requested synthetic rows without failing. Receipt content, data files, membership, source revision, requested count, and mix proportion are not enforced. See logic `replay_builder`; shell replay cases. |
| **M8 — reasoning-only actions** | **Partially fixed** | Explicit unfinished `<think>…` is rejected. Actual AW `step()` with empty content and an action only in `reasoning_content` records an invalid output and executes nothing. However, Qwen's thinking prompt already contains the opening tag, which is sliced away from the saved completion. A bare JSON action generated before any `</think>` still passes **both** parsers. See logic `templates_and_parser`, `aw_reasoning_only`. |
| **M9 — strict JSON/coordinate validation** | **Partially fixed** | Duplicate keys, NaN/Infinity, booleans, and fractional coordinates are rejected by strict parsing; lenient nonfinite coordinates now return no action, and canonical integer coordinates pass. A separate malformed scroll direction still raises from both parsers and aborts consumers; see new major issue below. Evidence: logic parser probes; edge-case results. |
| **M10 — teardown/lifecycle failures** | **Partially fixed** | Teardown exceptions are now visible in summaries. A synthetic successful task whose teardown raises still retains reward 1.0; an explicit `exception_info` plus reward 1.0 also remains successful. Cleanup still accepts an error-status response with empty stdout. See residual checks `patched_teardown`, `cleanup_error_status_empty_stdout`; logic AW summaries. These are downstream AW issues, not claims about a Qwen GPU run. |
| **M11 — AW denominator/numeric handling** | **Partially fixed** | With `--expected 116`, one success yields **1/116**, 115 missing episodes, and completed-only success 1.0. A teardown failure is counted. List-valued pickle records still discard all but the first entry; NaN step counts and infinite rewards survive into JSON; exceptions do not force reward zero; no schedule identity/deduplication is checked. See logic `aw_summaries`; residual AW summaries. |
| **M12 — provenance** | **Partially fixed** | `run_config.json` now includes model/dataset SHA strings and six package versions. A stub returning revision A for metadata and revision B for the subsequent load proves those recorded SHAs are **not used as load revisions**. Local models are labeled `local`; replay membership, source/template hashes, and evaluation resource identities are missing. See logic `provenance` and generation manifests. |
| **M13 — privileged offline history** | **Not fixed — accepted scope** | Documented privileged history remains. It is not used as a new launch blocker. Offline episode metrics must retain their teacher-forced interpretation. |
| **m1 — upload inventory/namespaces** | **Partially fixed** | AC's new direct partial uploader has no wrapper size filter. The wrapper still excludes a 500,000,000-byte current raw file while uploading an unrelated 499,999,999-byte file. No complete inventory is verified. See residual checks `upload_size_boundary`. |
| **m2 — retry/legacy telemetry** | **Not fixed** | Current summarization still reports zero first-attempt validity/calls for legacy records without those fields. New two-attempt fixtures count two calls but aggregate only the final top-level usage. See residual checks `aw_summaries.legacy_no_attempts` and `.two_attempts`. |
| **m3 — small schema/metric edges** | **Not fixed** | Source still has the coordinate endpoint mismatch, CLI JSON-decoding of an already decoded bbox, and lenient parsing in the geometrically named `click_bbox_hit_strict` metric. No fix was claimed; these are source-confirmed residuals, not newly reproduced launch blockers. |
| **m4 — documentation overclaims** | **Not fixed** | The general-eval docstring still says 500 questions/first-letter parsing. AW documentation says exceptions count as failures despite the reproduced positive rewards. Frozen GT contains 8,444 nonterminal high rows, **8,428** nonterminal low rows, and 1,543 terminals, rather than the dataset documentation's full doubled-step count. |

The on-disk GT file itself currently matches its sidecar: **18,415 unique keys**, SHA-256 `1926a44e0ffa7940b28866907d28913a5bd4af38f5b67463798cec8b6df25e84`. Its 1,543 episode IDs equal `data/meta/splits.json`'s test IDs. This verifies local consistency, not the contents of a live Hub dataset. The defect is that the scorer never enforces the SHA: modifying GT without changing the sidecar, or deleting the sidecar, still produces exit 0. Prediction labels being ignored is a real and useful fix.

**Critical residuals affecting the chains.**

At [vm_model_pipeline.sh:60](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/scripts/vm_model_pipeline.sh:60), the replacement gate is still `-f`, now applied to `train_receipt.json`. Neither reuse gate reads it. The validator at [sft_vlm.py:236](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/train/sft_vlm.py:236) does not resolve a safetensors index, validate adapter size/config, require the full processor assets, compare step completion, or bind files to arguments. A torn zero-byte receipt authorizes reuse. Likewise, an existing metrics filename authorizes skipping evaluation even when its contents say `complete:false`. Synthetic shell success demonstrates these control-flow decisions, not successful loading or uploading of a corrupt real model.

At [vm_model_pipeline.sh:21](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/scripts/vm_model_pipeline.sh:21) and [:71](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/scripts/vm_model_pipeline.sh:71), upload failures never call `fail()`. The reproduction makes **every** upload subprocess fail and still receives exit 0. Ordinary stage failures now reach the runner correctly, but [chain_next_job.sh:16](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/scripts/chain_next_job.sh:16) still accepts any `"exit …"` status with no expected run identity. With `{"status":{"unrelated-or-stale-job":"exit 7"}}`, it uploads and launches the next Qwen job, returning 0. Failed uploads and a fake background client that immediately exits 23 also produce a successful launch announcement. There is no verified startup acknowledgement.

These are not speculative GPU failures. They are executed exit-status and file-reuse counterexamples in the launch path.

**New critical regression — partial restore can give old predictions a new identity.**

The new restore block at [generate.py:61](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/eval/androidcontrol/generate.py:61) moves the JSONL into place **before** obtaining its manifest. In the reproduction, the JSONL download succeeds and the manifest download raises `FileNotFoundError`. The broad exception handler continues. Lines 74–75 then write the current manifest; lines 90–107 reuse old rows solely by key.

The restored first row retained `config_hash="old-thinking-policy"`, while the new manifest described the current configuration. Generation performed zero new calls and exited 0. The actual scorer also exited **0**, reporting complete coverage and 100% on this synthetic two-row set. An upload exception is similarly treated as a successful generation process.

Existing local predictions without a manifest take the same unsafe path. Checking equality of two manifest dictionaries is insufficient when missing metadata is manufactured and row hashes are ignored. Additionally, `min_pixels` is not in the manifest, so a change from 65,536 to 262,144 reused every existing row. See [full restore evidence](pass3/generation_v6drr5w7/results.json), especially `partial_restore_missing_manifest` and `restored_stale_rows_score`.

Require an existing valid identity before accepting any existing row, validate each row against it, and restore the prediction/manifest pair from a single verified Hub revision into staging before publication. Verify the GT sidecar hash as part of scoring. A missing sidecar must not be repaired by assigning a current identity to old outputs.

**New major protocol gap — the matched no-think branch is never certified.**

[vm_model_pipeline.sh:96](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/scripts/vm_model_pipeline.sh:96) adds the correct generation policy, but only `touch`es a `.done` file after generation. The two current Qwen jobs each invoke generation three times and scoring only twice. No matched FT metrics file is produced. Running that high-only output through the new scorer against full GT returns **2**, because all low-mode keys are absent.

`--allow_incomplete` can produce exploratory numbers, but it does not certify a complete predefined high-only subset, and it also bypasses model/duplicate integrity failures. Define and verify the expected high-mode key set, score the matched output, and create completion metadata only after that succeeds. Base's high-mode result then has an explicit matched FT counterpart.

**New major residual defect — malformed scroll arguments crash scoring and AW.**

Both of these valid JSON strings raise an uncaught `TypeError`:

```json
{"action_type":"scroll","direction":[]}
{"action_type":"scroll","direction":{}}
```

[agent_format.py:164](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/train/agent_format.py:164) and [:230](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/train/agent_format.py:230) test membership in a set before checking that direction is a string. The scorer exits **1** without metrics. The actual AW `step()` escapes after its first query, before recording the attempt or reaching its permitted retry. Pass-2 code produces the same exceptions, so this is **newly identified, not introduced by the fix**. Validate argument types and return an invalid action within the recorded parse-failure boundary. Evidence: [parser, scorer, and AW reproductions](pass3/edge_cases_apjhmgt5/results.json).

**Qwen path and hand-off verification.**

| Path/configuration | Executed outcome |
|---|---|
| `qwen35_2b_l4.json` | Generic pipeline receives the configured model, 1250 steps, BS8/GA4, template, and target regex. Both AC scorers receive an existing mapped `/content/work/meta/androidcontrol_test_gt.jsonl` through `--gt`. All AC generation calls and base/FT general calls receive 774400 pixels. |
| `qwen35_08b_l4.json` | Generic pipeline receives the configured model, 48000-row limit, one epoch, replay, BS16/GA2, template, and regex. Same valid GT and pixel argument routing as 2B. |
| Regex/template controls | Shell argument expansion preserves each configured regex. Against the previously pinned local model-source reference, each matches all **186** enumerated LM linear modules and no enumerated vision modules. Jinja training markers retain assistant reasoning/action and exclude prompts; the patch round-trip succeeds. This is not a real PEFT/CUDA training batch. |
| Same-session chain | Attempts GT upload to the correct `/content/work/meta/…jsonl` destination, but **does not create `meta`**, upload the GT manifest, check upload success, or run bootstrap. A fake CLI refusing the GT upload because its parent is missing still leads to launch. Whether a real CLI auto-creates that directory is not established. |
| Replacement-session chain | Fresh launcher does create `work/meta` and checks bootstrap failure. It uploads the GT JSONL, **not its SHA manifest**. This path still lacks verified background startup. |
| Replacement VM, 2B prerequisites | `PRE_GENERAL_MODELS` still requires `/content/runs/lfm3vl2b-ac-lora/merged`. No hand-off restores that directory. With that path absent, the actual wrapper records the prerequisite failure, runs subsequent stubbed Qwen stages, and exits **1**. Resolve the previous FT artifact from durable storage before launching on a replacement VM. |
| `qwen35_train_l4.json` (legacy Qwen entry point) | Still selects `vm_train_eval_push.sh`. Its inherited training `EXTRA` reaches `generate.py`, whose actual parser exits **2** for `--chat_template_path`/`--target_regex`. Even bypassing that earlier defect, `vm_eval.sh` supplies no `--gt`: flattened `score.py` defaults to **`/data/meta/androidcontrol_test_gt.jsonl`**, not `work/meta`. A shell fixture reaching this scorer ends `FT EVAL DONE rc=1` then `PIPELINE DONE`, exit **0**. |

The missing default-GT location is a **new major compatibility regression for callers left on `vm_eval.sh`** after deploying the new scorer. It does **not** affect the explicit `--gt` calls in the two current generic Qwen jobs. The legacy training `EXTRA` leakage is a separate pre-existing defect. Keep that distinction when labeling results or selecting a job JSON; `qwen35_*.json` files are not interchangeable. Evidence: [shell traces](pass3/shell_argr928_/results.json), [legacy generator CLI rejection](pass3/regressions_naszv_j0/results.json).

**Qwen thinking-output validity still blocks a trustworthy deployment-policy score.**

The real Jinja prompt for thinking-enabled FT ends with `<think>\n`. [generate.py:123](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/eval/androidcontrol/generate.py:123) saves only tokens after the input. If the completion is `{"action_type":"wait"}` and ends before `</think>`, the parser receives that bare object and accepts it as a final action, including in strict mode. The new literal `<think>` check cannot detect a tag present only in the prefill. Track the effective thinking state/prefix and require closure before accepting a final action. The separate AW removal of the `reasoning_content` fallback is verified and should be retained.

**Launch decision: NO-GO for both chains.**

Before launch, make success depend on verified matching receipts and scored artifacts; propagate mandatory upload failures; make the chain require fresh, expected-job success and checked uploads/startup; restore and validate prediction identities atomically; enforce the GT SHA; certify the matched high-only evaluation; and reject unfinished-prefill reasoning and malformed action arguments. The 2B replacement-VM path additionally needs its previous-model prerequisite restored or explicitly rescheduled. The legacy Qwen job JSON should be repaired or clearly excluded from the chain choices.

M13 privileged history, the older scripts already running LFM3/450M jobs, and the known manifest-less legacy base file remain accepted limitations. They were **not** treated as newly introduced blockers, and this report makes no claim about those live runs. AW lifecycle/numeric issues and deferred paired-CI analysis remain reporting requirements even after the Qwen launch path is repaired.
#!/bin/bash
# Wait for the current blocking-cell job on <session> to finish, then launch <next_jobs.json> on the same
# session (fresh code re-uploaded first). If the session is gone, allocate a new L4 and bootstrap it.
# Usage: COLAB_BIN=... COLAB_CONFIG=... HF_TOKEN_FILE=... scripts/chain_next_job.sh <session> <next_jobs.json>
set -u
set -o pipefail
SESS=$1; NEXT=$2
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
CB=${COLAB_BIN:-$HOME/.local/bin/colab}
C() { "$CB" ${COLAB_CONFIG:+--config "$COLAB_CONFIG"} "$@" < /dev/null 2> >(grep -v "new version\|colab update\|silence this" >&2); }
ST=$(mktemp)
echo "[$(date +%T)] waiting for current job on $SESS"
while true; do
  OUTP=$(C download -s "$SESS" /content/work/jobs_status.json "$ST" 2>&1 || true)
  if echo "$OUTP" | grep -q "lost\|not found\|404"; then
    echo "[$(date +%T)] session $SESS gone"; GONE=1; break
  fi
  RID=$(python3 -c "import json;print(json.load(open('$ST')).get('run_id','none'))" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
  if grep -q '"running"' "$ST" 2>/dev/null; then SEEN_RID=${SEEN_RID:-$RID}; fi
  # only an exit of the SAME run we saw running counts (stale statuses from earlier runs are ignored)
  if [ -n "${SEEN_RID:-}" ] && [ "$RID" = "$SEEN_RID" ] && grep -q '"exit' "$ST" 2>/dev/null && ! grep -q '"running"' "$ST"; then
    echo "[$(date +%T)] job finished: $(cat $ST)"
    grep -qE '"exit [1-9]' "$ST" && echo "WARNING: predecessor job FAILED; launching the next (independent) model anyway"
    GONE=0; break
  fi
  sleep 120
done
if [ "$GONE" = 1 ]; then
  export COLAB_CLI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=900
  for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do C new -s "$SESS" --gpu L4 && break; sleep 60; done
  bash scripts/launch_gpu_jobs.sh "$SESS" "$NEXT"
else
  echo 'import os;os.makedirs("/content/work/meta",exist_ok=True);print("meta ok")' | "$CB" ${COLAB_CONFIG:+--config "$COLAB_CONFIG"} exec -s "$SESS" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "meta ok" || { echo "cannot reach $SESS kernel"; exit 1; }
  for f in train/sft_vlm.py train/agent_format.py train/qwen35_chat_template_gen.jinja eval/androidcontrol/generate.py \
           eval/androidcontrol/score.py eval/general/eval_general.py data/build_general_replay.py scripts/vm_model_pipeline.sh \
           scripts/vm_blocking_jobs.py scripts/vm_eval.sh scripts/vm_train.sh scripts/colab_bootstrap.py data/meta/androidcontrol_test_gt.jsonl data/meta/androidcontrol_test_gt.manifest.json; do
    dst="/content/work/$(basename "$f")"; [[ "$f" == data/meta/* ]] && dst="/content/work/meta/$(basename "$f")"
    [ -f "$f" ] || { echo "missing $f"; exit 1; }
    C upload -s "$SESS" "$f" "$dst" | grep -q Uploaded || { echo "upload failed: $f"; exit 1; }
  done
  C upload -s "$SESS" "$NEXT" /content/work/jobs.json | grep -q Uploaded || { echo "upload failed: jobs.json"; exit 1; }
  nohup "$CB" ${COLAB_CONFIG:+--config "$COLAB_CONFIG"} exec -s "$SESS" --timeout 200000 -f scripts/vm_blocking_jobs.py \
    > "logs/${SESS}_jobs_cell_next.log" 2>&1 < /dev/null &
  PID=$!
  sleep 90
  if grep -q "heartbeat" "logs/${SESS}_jobs_cell_next.log" && kill -0 $PID 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "[$(date +%T)] launched $NEXT on $SESS (pid $PID) — heartbeat confirmed"
  else
    echo "[$(date +%T)] LAUNCH NOT CONFIRMED: $(tail -c 300 logs/${SESS}_jobs_cell_next.log)"; exit 1
  fi
fi
"""Run ON the Colab VM via `colab exec -f` (with a large --timeout).

Colab only keeps a VM alive while its Jupyter kernel is busy, so long jobs must run
INSIDE a kernel cell: this script launches the job scripts listed in
/content/work/jobs.json as subprocesses (logs to files) and blocks until all exit,
printing a heartbeat every 60 s. Monitor from outside with `colab download` of the logs.

jobs.json: [{"name": "train", "script": "/content/work/vm_train.sh", "env": {...}, "log": "/content/runs/x.log"}, ...]
"""
import json
import os
import subprocess
import time

jobs = json.load(open("/content/work/jobs.json"))
import uuid
RUN_ID = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]  # lets watchers tie an exit status to the run they saw start
procs = []
for j in jobs:
    env = dict(os.environ, **{k: str(v) for k, v in j.get("env", {}).items()})
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(j["log"]), exist_ok=True)
    p = subprocess.Popen(["bash", j["script"]], cwd="/content/work", env=env,
                         stdout=open(j["log"], "a"), stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
    procs.append((j["name"], p))
    print(f"started {j['name']} pid={p.pid}", flush=True)
    time.sleep(j.get("stagger_s", 0))

t0 = time.time()
while True:
    alive = [(n, p) for n, p in procs if p.poll() is None]
    status = {n: ("running" if p.poll() is None else f"exit {p.returncode}") for n, p in procs}
    with open("/content/work/jobs_status.json", "w") as f:
        json.dump({"run_id": RUN_ID, "elapsed_s": round(time.time() - t0), "status": status}, f)
    print(f"[heartbeat {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S')}] {status}", flush=True)
    if not alive:
        break
    for _ in range(6):
        time.sleep(10)
        print(".", end="", flush=True)  # keep the exec websocket busy (idle drops seen ~30-40s)
failed = [n for n, p in procs if p.returncode != 0]
print("ALL JOBS FINISHED", "FAILED: " + ",".join(failed) if failed else "OK", flush=True)
if failed:
    raise SystemExit(1)

